ed as the most common, primary, and most effective treatment for most cases, typically performed under general anesthesia through a small front-of-neck incision; it may involve lobectomy or total thyroidectomy and sometimes lymph node removal. Surgical findings and postoperative pathology guide whether additional SURGERY or adjuvant therapy (such as radiation or targeted therapy) is needed, especially when the cancer cannot be fully removed or other options are ineffective.\n\nSURGERY is also a mainstay for **non-melanoma skin cancers** such as cutaneous squamous cell skin cancer (CSCC) and basal cell carcinoma, where it aims to remove the tumor while minimizing damage to surrounding skin, using techniques such as standard excision, re-excision for margins, Mohs surgery, and other margin-assessment approaches, sometimes requiring reconstruction/closure. For **anal cancer**, SURGERY is included among modalities used by experienced teams and may involve significant considerations around complications, pain, recovery, and where it is performed. For **colon and rectal cancers**, SURGERY (including colectomy and other procedures) is a key treatment, helps confirm extent of disease, and can drive surveillance planning; pelvic MRI may be ordered when SURGERY is being considered for rectal cancer staging and planning. For **bladder cancer**, SURGERY ranges from small resections to removal of the entire bladder and nearby organs in advanced cases.\n\nIn **glioma**, SURGERY is often the main treatment to remove as much tumor as possible, but complete removal may be difficult because tumors can blend into normal brain tissue; some low-grade tumors may be managed with SURGERY alone, while higher-grade disease commonly requires additional therapy. By contrast, SURGERY is generally **not** a standard option for **primary CNS lymphoma** because tumors may be deep and surgical removal can be risky and more harmful than helpful. In **head and neck cancers**, SURGERY may be preferred